't it hasn't passed that  test today and fatima the government  might argue that this latest oil field  abigail contains only 5.5 million  barrels of oil equivalent it's not huge  and they would say  we feel we can tolerate these projects  and still meet our climate targets which  are ambitious  but this project doesn't even touch the  sides if we're talking about energy  security or providing energy for the  taxpayers it's just simply not going to  provide or solve any of the problems  that we have so this is doesn't make  business sense it doesn't make economic  sense i mean it's really hard to  understand and make the case that this  energy project is one that's worth  investing in and it's worth taking the  risk of us furthering uh  falling further into the climate crisis  because it actually isn't solving any of  the problems that the climate is that  the government is saying that it's  trying to solve so it's a bad business  project okay simply even if we don't  care about the climate and very very  briefly michael um the government would  say it can hit its targets they will  point out where look at the amount of  money that's going into wind power in  this country for example we're doing the  right thing there  we are doing the right thing on wind  power although there's not enough going  into onshore wind but the simple fact  remains that new exploration of oil and  gas increases the global total of carbon  dioxide and other greenhouse gas  emissions and that is not possible  within the budgets that the government  has set itself to it's not just about  this country it's about the global total  we're very unlikely to consume much of  the energy from this new field it'll go  into the global supply and that is  exceeding the carbon budget that will  allow global temperature to be uh kept  to safe levels and that is the simple  hard fact of climate change as we're  experiencing it now i'm going to crack  on with the next subject now if that's  all right because we want to talk about  a freedom of information request from  the carbon brief website which today  revealed details of a briefing on  climate change given to boris johnson  shortly after his arrival in downing  street now the chief scientific advisor  patrick valance laid out the science of  global warming the role of carbon and  the devastating likely future impacts  and now we're not all lucky enough to  have a personal briefing from the  country's top scientists so what is the  best way to get through to people  whether that's world leaders or the  public at large so fatima yeah we  haven't all got the chief scientific  advisor to run us through a few slides  have we so what do you think the best  way to communicate the message on  climate change is  yeah i mean i had a wonderful  conversation today with the  the head of the institute of physics  about how you know we need to be better  about communicating the nuances of  climate science we can sometimes you  know extrapolate top lines from the  climate science that uh don't build  trust with the public um whether it's  like we've got 10 years left to save the  world or not even communicating using  the right terms talking about 1.5  degrees when 1.5 degrees doesn't mean  anything to the average person and so i  think we could be doing better about  taking the time to explain you know the  risks that are posed by inaction but  also talking about the solutions  pointing towards the vision of the  future that people can tangibly see and  not just speaking about the problems and  the wealth of knowledge that exists in  both science and technology about what  the future can look like and we can do  that by working with scientists and we  could the media could also be better  platforming science scientists uh  because they are great communicators and  they are trustworthy communicators i  think the film don't look up was a  really great example of how you know  scientists are currently just stuck in  this room  know the evidence have the facts and are  just trying to get it out there and we  could be platforming them the scientists  are trusted across the public maybe more  so than you know activists are  that's that's so interesting and getting  the message over to the public is  clearly really important michael isn't  it but also to to politicians and you  know all about advising politicians  don't you so what's the way to get your  message across to those who can play a  real role in shaping policy  yeah i think it depends very much on  their prior level of knowledge i was a  bit shocked when i looked at these uh  slides and the email exchanges that  accompanied them well done to carbon  brief the freedom of information request  to get hold of them they were really  climate science 101 this was not about  the economic costs and benefits of  different technologies and solutions to  the climate crisis this was really about  the fact that greenhouse gas emissions  cause temperature to rise and  temperature rises  have huge devastating environmental and  social effects and i was really rather  shocked that the prime minister needed  such a basic introduction to climate  science and climate impacts um when he  became prime minister really we should  expect our politicians to be better  informed but well done for him for  asking for that briefing well done for  the scientists to explain it to him and  it obviously had some effect he changed  his view and in the end we've got to  live with the politicians we have at  least for the moment and we've got to  work with them uh to get them not just  to understand the science but also  themselves how to communicate it in ways  that they feel okay will bring the  public along okay that's the  in the end the point the politicians  will respond to the public so it's also  about how we talk to the public okay  yeah and a very brief last thought on  that fatima some people just don't want  to hear the message especially when it  can seem quite grim  i'm just going to end with what michael  said which is you know boris johnson is  a populist why did he ask for this  report because when he became prime  minister in 2019 we had this map mass  mobilizations he knew the public was  concerned about uh climate change he  wanted to know more and be seen to be  acting on it so actually the best way we  convince our leaders is by taking to the  streets and showing them what's the most  important priority we want them to act  on okay we're going to leave it there  fatima ibrahim michael jacobs really  interesting to get your take on things  thanks very much indeed
 

